---
import Divider from "../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const FirstProgram = `console.log("Ready.");`;

const FirstProgramOut = `Ready.`;

const SyntaxErrorCode = `console.log("Ready.);`;

const SyntaxErrorOut = `1 | console.log("Ready.);
                ^
error: Unterminated string literal
    at C:\\Users\\you\\js-workspace\\activities\\app.js:1:13`;

const NameErrorCode = `console2.log("Ready.");`;

const NameErrorOut = `1 | console2.log("Ready.");
    ^
ReferenceError: console2 is not defined
      at C:\\Users\\you\\js-workspace\\activities\\app.js:1:1`;

const RuntimeErrorCode = `let crew = ["Ada", "Bo"];
let name = crew[5];

console.log(name.length);`;

const RuntimeErrorOut = `TypeError: undefined is not an object (evaluating 'name.length')`;

const LabStarter = `function setup()
{
    createCanvas(400, 400);
    background(50);
}`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Setting Up Your Workspace</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="what-you-need">
          <h2>What You Need</h2>

          <p>
            Two things: <b class="text-sn-theme">VS Code</b> and{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Bun</b>. On a lab computer both are already
            installed and you can skip straight to the next section.
          </p>

          <p>
            Everything else arrives in one folder that you download once. The
            libraries this course uses are already inside it, so there is nothing
            to install, and <b>nothing in this course needs an internet
            connection</b> once you have the folder.
          </p>

          <p class="text-secondary">
            Working on your own computer? Install VS Code from{" "}
            <a href="https://code.visualstudio.com/" target="_blank"
              >code.visualstudio.com</a
            > and Bun from <a href="https://bun.sh/" target="_blank">bun.sh</a>,
            following the instructions there for your operating system. Get{" "}
            <b>Bun 1.3.14</b> if you are offered a choice &mdash; error messages
            change wording between versions, and that is the one this course is
            written against.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="get-the-workspace">
          <h2>Get the Workspace</h2>

          <ol>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Download it.</b>{" "}
              <a href="/workspaces/js-workspace.zip" class="text-sn-theme"
                >js-workspace.zip</a
              >
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Unzip it.</b> Right-click the file and choose <i
                >Extract All</i
              >. You want the folder somewhere you can find again &mdash; your
              Documents folder is fine, your Downloads folder is not.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Open the folder in VS Code.</b> <i>File &gt; Open Folder</i>,
              then pick the <CodeSnippet code="js-workspace" lang="txt" inline />{" "}
              folder itself. Not the folder above it, and not a file inside it.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Say yes to the extensions.</b> VS Code will offer to install
              four recommended extensions. Click <i>Install</i>.{" "}
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Nothing in this course works properly
              until you do</b>, and the offer only appears once.
            </li>
          </ol>

          <p class="text-secondary">
            If you missed the extension prompt, open the Extensions panel and
            type <CodeSnippet code="@recommended" lang="txt" inline /> in its
            search box. The same four will be listed.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="first-program">
          <h2>Your First Program</h2>

          <p>
            Open <CodeSnippet code="activities/app.js" lang="txt" inline />,
            delete what is in it, and type this:
          </p>

          <Code code={FirstProgram} lang="javascript" frame="code" title="app.js" />

          <p>
            Press the <b class="text-sn-theme">Run</b> button &mdash; the ▶ at
            the top right. A terminal panel opens at the bottom and shows:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={FirstProgramOut}
            lang="txt"
            frame="terminal"
            title="Terminal"
            showLineNumbers={false}
          />

          <p>
            That is the whole loop for this course: write, press Run, read what
            came out.
          </p>

          <p class="text-secondary">
            <CodeSnippet code="app.js" lang="txt" inline /> is a scratchpad.
            Use it to try things out. Your actual activity work goes in files
            named the way each activity page tells you.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="two-ways-to-run">
          <h2>Two Ways to Run, and They Are Not Interchangeable</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Terminal programs</b> &mdash; anything in{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="activities" lang="txt" inline /> or{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="challenges" lang="txt" inline />. Open the file
            and press <b>Run</b>. Output appears in the terminal panel, and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="prompt()" lang="javascript" inline /> will wait
            for you to type there.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Labs</b> &mdash; the p5.js drawing work in{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="labs" lang="txt" inline />. Right-click{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="labs/index.html" lang="txt" inline /> and choose{" "}
            <i>Open with Live Server</i>. A browser tab opens and refreshes
            itself every time you save.
          </p>

          <div class="alert alert-warning mt-3">
            <b>The Run button does nothing useful on a lab file.</b> Bun has no
            canvas to draw on, so you get an empty terminal and no error message
            &mdash; which looks exactly like a broken program when it is only the
            wrong button. If you press Run on a file like this and nothing
            happens, that is why:
            <Code
              code={LabStarter}
              lang="javascript"
              frame="code"
              title="labs/app.js"
              showLineNumbers={false}
              class="mt-3"
            />
          </div>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="where-work-goes">
          <h2>Where Your Work Goes</h2>

          <table class="table table-dark table-striped">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Folder</th><th>What goes in it</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><CodeSnippet code="activities" lang="txt" inline /></td>
                <td>Your activity programs, one file each, named the way the activity page says</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><CodeSnippet code="labs" lang="txt" inline /></td>
                <td>The p5.js labs. You edit <CodeSnippet code="app.js" lang="txt" inline /> and sometimes <CodeSnippet code="index.html" lang="txt" inline /></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><CodeSnippet code="challenges" lang="txt" inline /></td>
                <td>Your code challenge programs</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <div class="alert alert-info">
            <b>Keep everything you write.</b> In May, the Create Performance Task
            asks you to write about a program you built &mdash; from memory,
            without the code in front of you. The work you keep now is what you
            will have to draw on then. <b class="text-sn-theme">Do not overwrite
            old activities.</b>
          </div>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="reading-errors">
          <h2>Reading an Error</h2>

          <p>
            You will see three kinds this year, and telling them apart is most of
            debugging. <b class="text-sn-theme">Read the message before you
            change anything.</b>
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">Nothing runs at all</h3>

          <p>A missing quote, bracket, or parenthesis:</p>

          <Code code={SyntaxErrorCode} lang="javascript" frame="code" title="app.js" showLineNumbers={false} />
          <Code code={SyntaxErrorOut} lang="txt" frame="terminal" title="Terminal" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-2" />

          <p>
            Bun shows you the line, puts a <CodeSnippet code="^" lang="txt" inline />{" "}
            under roughly where it gave up, and names the file, line and column
            at the end. <b>Nothing printed</b>, because the program never
            started.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">A name the program does not know</h3>

          <Code code={NameErrorCode} lang="javascript" frame="code" title="app.js" showLineNumbers={false} />
          <Code code={NameErrorOut} lang="txt" frame="terminal" title="Terminal" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-2" />

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet code="ReferenceError" lang="txt" inline /> means you
            used a name that does not exist &mdash; usually a typo, sometimes a
            variable you never made.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">It started, then stopped</h3>

          <Code code={RuntimeErrorCode} lang="javascript" frame="code" title="app.js" showLineNumbers={false} />
          <Code code={RuntimeErrorOut} lang="txt" frame="terminal" title="Terminal" showLineNumbers={false} class="mt-2" />

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet code="TypeError" lang="txt" inline /> means the program
            asked something to do a thing it cannot do. Here{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="crew[5]" lang="javascript" inline /> does not
            exist, so <CodeSnippet code="name" lang="javascript" inline /> is{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="undefined" lang="javascript" inline />, and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="undefined" lang="javascript" inline /> has no
            length.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The tell is whether anything printed first.</b>{" "}
            Output and then a crash means the program ran and hit something part
            way through. No output at all means it never got started.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="troubleshooting">
          <h2>If Something Goes Wrong</h2>

          <dl>
            <dt class="text-sn-theme mt-3">The Run button is not there</dt>
            <dd>
              The Code Runner extension did not install. Open the Extensions
              panel, search <CodeSnippet code="@recommended" lang="txt" inline />,
              and install all four.
            </dd>

            <dt class="text-sn-theme mt-3">
              An import on line 1 fails
            </dt>
            <dd>
              You opened the wrong folder. VS Code has to be opened at{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="js-workspace" lang="txt" inline /> itself, not
              at the folder containing it. Close it and reopen with{" "}
              <i>File &gt; Open Folder</i>.
            </dd>

            <dt class="text-sn-theme mt-3">
              I pressed Run and got an empty terminal
            </dt>
            <dd>
              Either you are on a lab file (see above), or your program defines
              things without ever calling them. Both produce no output and no
              error.
            </dd>

            <dt class="text-sn-theme mt-3">
              Colored text comes out plain
            </dt>
            <dd>
              That is expected anywhere except the terminal panel. Color only
              appears when the output is going to a real terminal &mdash; send it
              anywhere else and the styling silently disappears. It is not a bug
              in your program.
            </dd>

            <dt class="text-sn-theme mt-3">
              The lab page is blank
            </dt>
            <dd>
              Check that you opened <CodeSnippet code="index.html" lang="txt" inline />{" "}
              with Live Server and not the <CodeSnippet code="app.js" lang="txt" inline />{" "}
              next to it. The HTML file is what loads the drawing library.
            </dd>

            <dt class="text-sn-theme mt-3">Something else</dt>
            <dd>
              Tell me, and say what you pressed and what appeared. A setup
              problem is not something you should spend the period on.
            </dd>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Ready to Start
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
